foreign  professor of Science and religion at  Oxford University so you studied science  at University partly out of a desire for  certainty you wrote in your article for  II news how did the discovery of the  history and philosophy of science change  your mind about how much certainty there  actually is in science  well I was studying science in the sixth  form of school I I took the view science  was the most reliable way we had  understanding our world and not  something that was reliable that it gave  us certain knowledge of things and I  like that idea you know we all want to  be sure about things then I read a book  by Bertrand Russell the history of  Western philosopher and he said this and  it made me rethink he said the task of  philosophy is to teach us how to live  with uncertainty without being paralyzed  by hesitation and Burton Russell  Wilson's job you know he clearly had  seen that there was an issue here that  in many areas of life we had to in  effect recognize we couldn't have  certainty and that really opened the way  to thinking about this more so I looked  at the history and philosophy of science  and Russell was right you know in effect  science is very very good at pointing us  towards good answers but didn't always  get us there  but even if there isn't like a hundred  percent certainty within science  um does that mean that you would put  more certainty on like you you'd see  certain um evidence or certain  scientific  um narratives as being more certain than  other narratives given that it's more  it's an empirical sign yeah I think  science at the moment is dominant by the  philosophy of inference to the best  explanation and that means you look at  all the observations you say what are  the possible explanations and which  seems to be the best and you can usually  work that out the simplest or the most  elegant but the best isn't always the  right and the right maybe something it  hasn't come along yet so there's always  this element of hesitation this element  of uncertainties a lot of provisionality  in science which means that science is  on a journey and we're at this point in  the journey and in 10 years time being a  different point of things may look  different  there are obvious conflicts between the  narratives that science and religion  have on certain issues for instance with  the creation story  how do you reconcile the two well there  are indeed some conflicts between  science and religion for example in  relation to Creation narratives but  you've got to bear in mind that if I was  talking to a hundred years ago that we'd  be talking about the scientific  consensus that the world has always  existed there's no such thing as a  beginning and of course we're talking  about the beginnings of the universe and  you can see immediately that while a  scientific Narrative of origination and  the religious Narrative of creation are  not the same this kind of way  interesting space level of dialogue but  you're right I mean there are real  tensions between science and religion as  there are between every intellectual  discipline I see this as being creative  you know they open up really good  questions for discussion  so do you embrace one narrative or the  other or you're just interested in the  dialogue between them I try to inhabit  with the scientific and the religious  narrative and that means that some  sometimes I step into one sometimes step  into the other but I'm always asking how  can we bring these into conversation how  might they enrich each other so if you  like it's all about trying to develop a  bigger picture of life then either of  those can give on their own  and are there certain  um current scientific kind of accepted  truths that um or theories that you are  a bit skeptical of I think the model by  which we calculate the age of the  universe is open for radical revision  there are so many issues with it and  basically at the moment we all think the  universe is about nearly 14 billion  years old but that's not based on  observation that's about observations  being interpreted in the theoretical  context that theoretical context I think  is going to change over the next 20  years and then we may have a different  age for our universe which is very  exciting  it's very interesting and on a more  personal level you used to be an atheist  and then you converted to Christianity  what sparked this transformation this  change of mind well I used to be an  atheist when I was at high school and I  was studying science and it was really  wonderful but I took the view that  science and religion were  irreconciliably opposed and also I grew  up in Northern Ireland and in Northern  Ireland back in the 1960s we had  religious violence and so it seemed to  me if there was no religion then a  certain knowledge be no violence that's  a no-brainer so it seemed to me just  obvious that that was the way things  were I think I began to realize that  things weren't quite that simple for  example studying the philosophy of  science made me realize that actually in  many areas of life we have to make  decisions we cannot prove to be right  for example in ethics and politics and  so on I began to realize actually maybe  maybe Faith was something you had to get  used to because there are a lot of  things you just cannot prove and yet  they are so important that didn't  convert me or anything but it kind of  went together a bit more complicated  than I had originally thought and so I  began this process of exploration which  actually made me realize atheism at  least in my view wasn't as productive  and interesting as perhaps Christianity  was so I I moved from one to the other  but I don't anyway criticize atheism I  just say I think I found something  better and I'm happy to have a  conversation about this  um so what did you find in Christianity  that was more productive and better  that's a very good question what I find  in Christianity was a big picture of  life this idea that you stand back and  there is this bigger picture of which  each individual part is important that  gave me a framework for integration  science  religion ethics quest for meaning the  Quest for understanding is all part of  something that is in effect one and the  same and Jeffy like gave me this this  unified Vision which helped me to see my  own existence and also everybody else in  a new way so if you like it was an  expansion of my intellectual Horizons  and you mentioned um your your past view  that um you saw the violence in Northern  Ireland as um being sparked by religion  have you changed your mind about what  actually sparked that violence I think  although back in the 1964 this is  religion that's causing all this  violence I think when you look at more  closer they're clearly political and  economic issues there and maybe religion  is being used as a badge for some deeper  unease that gave rise to this violence  do you think that's the case with other  forms of violence that involve um  religion for instance in other areas of  the world  um like with terrorist groups  um using Islam and is it similarly the  case that you you see that as just a  badge being used for other  um other actual reasons for why this is  happening I think we're always looking  for a higher motivation for doing things  and it can be religion or it can be an  ideology like Marxism or Nazism and so  my concern really is to ensure that  people are able to hold political views  or religious views but not ones that  lead them to kind of extremist actions  that we're seeing in our world today I  don't know how we do that but it needs  to be done and actually there's no  necessary link between religion and  violence it seems to happen that we can  find some ways of breaking that link I'd  be a very happy man and then similarly  um to the question on science and what  you're skeptical of in current science  are you skeptical of certain ideas  within Christianity itself  I think I'm skeptical about everything  in the sense that I always want to know  why doesn't we think that well the  reason for thinking that and and some  beliefs I think can be challenged others  you challenge them I think actually that  seems quite robust in case of  Christianity I mean you're always saying  this is really the best way of making  sense of the New Testament or the Life  of Christ or whatever it is so there's  always that critical spirit but you have  to kind away realize you can't be a  Perpetual skeptic because you you don't  believe anything so what you're looking  for is always what seemed to be the most  reliable beliefs that you can use as a  basis for Meaningful existence in this  world  so if you were to give an example  if something is skeptical of in  Christianity or there isn't anything  within that narrative that um I think  there are always things in Christianity  or indeed in any worldview that you're  going to ask hard questions about and  for me I'd want to ask for example  whether the idea you find in so many  Christian thinkers that God cannot  suffer because God is perfect well I  mean where does that come from I mean it  seems to me that actually God cares this  world so much that God is actually  affected by the world he created  suffering in this way so there's a  conversation that has begun and I feel  quite strongly about it so you've talked  about  um the amazing impact that religion has  um in helping you kind of lead a more  productive kind of life and have a world  view that helps you move forward  um  would you see this as a rational or kind  of irrational use of of religion and to  what extent is religion rational in your  in your mind  I have a question of rationality in  religion is really interesting as indeed  the question of rationality as a whole  is really important one of the things  I've realized through my own research  but also just through reflection on this  is actually each area of life whether  it's science or ethics or religion  really develops its own distinct  rationalities way of approaching the  world its way of formulating questions  as ways of actually getting answers and  what I've realized is that each area of  our inquiry into the meaning of life or  the way the world Works uses different  methods and therefore although this may  sound strange I use one rationality when  I'm doing science another one I'm  thinking about ethics another way I'm  thinking about religion and I can't  always see these as necessary because of  their debt to Identity the topics and  the question really is how I can bring  these back together each method in  effect give me something I can build up  into a bigger picture of life so how are  these methods different and what do they  have in common then I think these  research methods are different and that  they're trying to relate the objects  under investigation so for example in  physics you'll have a very great  emphasis on the importance of the  empirical method as we look at our world  in ethics you're asking how can we live  a good life how what is good how do we  ask those questions in religion very  often what is the meaning of life and  how do I become fulfilled people now  these actually interlock these questions  you can imagine but actually you get to  the answers by different means so  science is not going to be able to tell  us what is good or indeed what the  meaning of life is any more than  religion could tell us how far the moon  was away what the value of Planck's  constant is so it's a question of in  effect recognizing each area has its own  distinct contribution to make but also  its limits how do we bring them together  to give us something that's meaningful  and helpful is there any idea you've  recently changed your mind on  like you asked a good question isn't it  um I used to think that there was this  thing called reason which was absolutely  homogeneous and irrespective of your  cultural context or what you were doing  it was the same thing I think in the  last five years I've realized it's not  that simple but actually reason is  culturally embedded it engages in  different questions in different ways  and in many ways the question is how can  we arrive at a framework for allowing us  to reason cross-culture it's a difficult  one but it's an important one and um is  there a thinker that has influenced you  a lot and would you be able to present  one of their ideas is in just under 10  minutes I've been influenced by many  thinkers and it's so wonderful to have  this but I'm going to single out Mary  Midgley the wonderful English  philosopher who died recently and it's  sorely missed out to say she was a  character a really interesting person  but also had some wonderful ideas and  she says look reality is so complex we  need multiple maps to try and make sense  of its different aspects you know  there's one map from science he's  another from ethics another religion and  we've got to try and do superimpose  those maps and get the most we can out  of them I think that's a wonderful idea  I'd love if unfortunately I can't do  this if she were here to ask her how do  we draw those Maps but I know what she  means and I think it's helpful drawing  Maps getting them right and then putting  them together and seeing the bigger  picture that emerges from those  individual Maps  for more debates talks and interviews  subscribe today to The Institute of Arts  and ideas at IAI TV

